  var NOTE={
    /* 2026-08 追加パターン */ "gown":"A robe you simply wrap and tie. One back, two fronts, two sleeves and a sash. No buttons, no zip — just knot the sash. Waffle or gauze makes a bathrobe; linen makes a summer cover-up.", "nocollarjacket":"An open-front jacket with no collar. Skipping the collar makes this the easiest jacket to sew. One back, two fronts, two sleeves and a facing. The neckline is finished with the facing. Try tweed or linen.", "cargopants":"Work pants with big patch pockets on the sides. Front and back legs, pockets and pocket flaps. The waist is elasticated, so there is no zip or button to fit. Chino or ripstop — anything with body.", "hanten":"A padded Japanese jacket. Every piece is a straight cut: two body panels (folded at the shoulder), two sleeves and a collar. Slip a layer of thin batting inside for real warmth. The fronts simply overlap and tie.", "kidsshirt":"A button-front shirt for children. Two fronts, one back, two sleeves and a collar. Three to five buttons is plenty. Great for nursery, school or weekends. Oxford, broadcloth or linen.", "nursingcape":"A cover to drape over you while feeding out and about. Gather the top edge and hang it from a neck strap. Thread wire through the top and the neckline stays open so you can see your baby. Double gauze or light cotton.", "strollerseat":"A one-piece liner for a stroller or car seat, with the back and seat cut as one. It protects the stroller from sweat and spills. Adjust the harness slot positions to match your own stroller. Gauze or toweling.", "pencase":"A boxy zip pencil case. Sew the single piece (folded at the base) into a tube and box the base corners — that is the whole thing. Interfacing makes it stand up, so it doubles as a pen pot. Canvas or corduroy.", "bankbook":"A bi-fold case that keeps passbooks, cards and a seal together. Body plus an inner pocket. Stitch the pocket vertically for card slots. An elastic band keeps the contents in. Scraps are plenty.", "flaskcover":"A shoulder cover with a lid for a water bottle. Base (circle), body, lid and a shoulder strap. Line it with insulating fabric and it keeps drinks cold. It also protects the bottle from knocks.", "randocover":"A cover for the flap of a Japanese school satchel. It keeps the leather dry and scratch-free, and reflective tape makes it visible at night. Single layer, with elastic at the lower edge and sides. Water-repellent or laminated fabric.", "recordercase":"A slim drawstring bag for a school recorder. Fold one piece in half, sew the sides and add a drawstring at the top. Add a name tag and it will never get mixed up. It also fits a melodica hose.", "nuiclothes":"A dress for a plush toy around 20 cm tall. One front, two backs and a skirt. The back closes with snaps or hook-and-loop. Measure your own toy's chest and length and enter them. Scraps are all you need.", "cosmepouch":"A trapezoid pouch with a wide base that opens up so you can see inside. Two body pieces plus a base. Interfacing or quilting makes it stand on its own, so make-up sits upright. Good for travel.", "passportcase":"A bi-fold case for passports, tickets, cards and local currency. Body plus two kinds of pocket. Stitch the pocket vertically for card slots. It comes out again every trip.", "laptopcase":"A padded sleeve for a laptop. Just one piece (folded at the base) with batting in between. Close it with hook-and-loop or a zip. Enter your own machine's measurements.", "tabletcase":"A tablet case with a flap. One piece (folded at the base) with batting inside, closed with a snap. Perfect for school tablets, and slim enough to fit inside a satchel.", "waistbag":"A small pouch to wear at the waist or across the body. Body (base on the fold), flap and a belt. Lengthen the belt and it becomes a sling bag. Hands-free for travel, walks and festivals.", "bostonbag":"A roomy bag for a night or two away. Two body panels, one continuous gusset (base and both sides in one strip) and two handles. Because the gusset is one strip, there are only two seams to join. Canvas or nylon.", "gadgetpouch":"A book-style pouch that keeps chargers and cables tidy. Body (base on the fold), mesh pockets and elastic loops. Open it and you can see everything at once.", "ehonbag":"The classic school tote with no gusset. One piece (base on the fold) plus two handles. Enter the size your nursery or school specifies. Quilted fabric keeps picture books from bending.", "petsling":"A crossbody sling for carrying a small dog or cat. Body (base on the fold), shoulder strap and a safety lead. Handy for vet trips and walks. Choose something strong and washable.", "petcarrier":"A boxy carrier for a small dog or cat. Two body panels, one continuous gusset, a mesh top and handles. The mesh top lets you see in and keeps the air moving. A stiff board in the base adds stability.", "tissuebox":"A cover for a box of tissues. Top (with the opening) plus four sides. It hides the packaging and tidies up the room. Add a hanging loop and it works on a wall or a car headrest.", "noren":"A Japanese split curtain for a doorway or as a screen. Fold the top edge into a rod pocket and leave the lower part open as slits. Straight seams only. Linen or cotton-linen suits both Japanese and Western rooms.", "remotepocket":"A caddy that hangs over a sofa arm for remotes and magazines. The panel on the inside acts as a counterweight so it does not slide off. Stitch the pocket vertically and each remote stands in its own slot.", "neckpillow":"A U-shaped cushion that supports your neck. Sew two pieces right sides together, turn and fill with stuffing or beads. Great for travel and naps in the car. Make a separate cover and you can wash it. Knit, fleece or toweling feels best.",

    /* 2026-07 追加パターン4 */ shirt:"A classic shirt with a front placket. Two fronts, one back, two sleeves and a collar. Hem it straight or curved. Best in oxford, broadcloth or linen — anything with a bit of body.", yukata:"Traditional Japanese summer wear, cut entirely from straight lines. Two body panels (folded at the shoulder), two sleeves, two front panels (okumi) and a collar. There is not a single curve, so it is approachable even if you have never sewn kimono. Sized around the traditional bolt width (about 36 cm). Cotton or cotton-linen. Every piece is a rectangle, so rather than printing (a yukata is long and would take many sheets) mark the measurements from the guide sheet straight onto the fabric.", samue:"A Japanese work jacket. One back, two fronts, two sleeves and ties. It fastens with ties at the sides, so it is easy to get on and off. Pair it with the tapered pants or jinbei shorts for a set. Cotton or sashiko weave.", kappogi:"A Japanese cover-all apron with sleeves that goes right over your clothes. Front and back bodice, two sleeves and ties. It ties at the back, and elastic at the cuffs keeps sleeves clean. Great for cooking, cleaning or school lunch duty.", pajamas:"A relaxed button-front pajama top. Two fronts, one back, two sleeves and a collar. Nothing binds, so it is comfortable to sleep in. Pair with the sweatpants or tapered pants for a set. Lovely in double gauze or flannel.", blouson:"A light jacket gathered into ribbing at the hem and cuffs. Two fronts, one back, two sleeves and ribbing. Close it with a zip or buttons. Nylon, twill or denim all work well.", diaperpouch:"A pouch that holds diapers and wipes together. Body (base on the fold), flap and a handle. Open the flap out and it doubles as a changing mat. Laminated fabric wipes clean in seconds.", wipescase:"A cover that slips over a shop-bought pack of baby wipes. A single layer with an opening cut for the wipes, fastened with hook-and-loop. It turns a disposable pack into something pretty, and you can add a lid. Try laminate or oxford cloth.", carriercover:"Covers that wrap the shoulder straps of a baby carrier to protect them from dribble. They fasten with hook-and-loop or snaps, so they go on and off easily. Gauze or toweling is absorbent and safe for a baby to chew.", cap:"A classic six-panel cap with a brim. Six panels plus two brim pieces. Sew the panels together in sequence to form the rounded crown. Add interfacing to the brim to hold its shape. Twill or denim.", boshitecho:"A bi-fold case that keeps a health record book, appointment cards and insurance cards together. Body plus inner pockets. Stitch the pocket vertically to create card slots. It works as a passbook case too.", cardcase:"A small bi-fold case for business or loyalty cards. Body plus an inner pocket. Sew the outer and lining right sides together, turn, and fold at the center. Interfacing gives it structure. Perfect for scraps.", bousaizukin:"A cover for a Japanese school disaster hood — it holds the hood and doubles as a chair-back cushion. Body plus elastic bands. Enter the size your school specifies. Sturdy quilted fabric is the usual choice.", dollclothes:"An A-line dress for a doll. Just two pieces, front and back. The back fastens with snaps or hook-and-loop. Measure your doll's chest and length and enter them. It uses very little fabric, so it is great practice too.", teddy:"A simple flat teddy bear. Two body pieces plus four ear pieces. Sew right sides together, turn through the gap and stuff. Try felt, fleece or corduroy. Embroider the eyes and nose rather than using buttons — safer for little ones.", scarf:"A fabric scarf — just fold a rectangle lengthwise and sew. Add a lining and it is reversible. In fleece you can skip finishing the edges entirely, or cut them into fringe. A lovely winter gift.", nametag:"A holder that attaches a name badge without making holes in clothing. Body plus a clip strip. Slip vinyl inside for a clear window you can slide the badge in and out of. A back-to-school essential.", wallet:"A bi-fold long wallet that takes notes flat. Body, card pockets and a coin section. Stitch the card pocket vertically to set how many slots you get. Interfacing keeps it in shape.", phonepouch:"A small crossbody pouch for your phone and a few essentials. Body (base on the fold), flap and a shoulder strap. Perfect for walks and errands hands-free. Enter your phone's size.", lunchbag:"A boxy bag that holds a lunch box upright. Body (base on the fold) plus handles. Line it with insulating fabric for summer. A deep gusset keeps everything stable and less likely to spill.", zabuton:"An envelope-style cover for a Japanese floor cushion. No zip needed — the back panels simply overlap. One front and two backs. Enter the size of your cushion. Every piece is a rectangle, so you can skip printing and cut straight from the measurements.", cafecurtain:"A short curtain for the lower half of a window or to screen a shelf. Just fold the top edge over to make a rod pocket. One rectangle, so it is a great first curtain project. Linen or lace.", coaster:"A five-minute coaster: sew the outer and lining right sides together and turn. Rounded corners are easier to turn and look softer. Ideal for using up scraps, and a nice little gift in a set.", ovenmitt:"A mitt-style pot holder you slide your hand into. Two layers of batting between the outer and lining keeps the heat out properly. Make it long enough to cover the wrist and reaching into the oven feels much safer.",

    /* 2026-07 追加パターン3 */ hoodie:"A pullover hoodie. Front and back bodice, two sleeves and two hood pieces. Best in French terry or sweatshirt fabric. Add ribbing at the cuffs and hem for a classic finish, and a front pocket if you like.", raglantee:"A T-shirt with raglan sleeves that run to the neckline. Front and back bodice plus two sleeves. The diagonal shoulder seam makes the sleeves easy to set in. Use a contrast fabric for the sleeves for the classic raglan look. Best in knit.", sweatpants:"Jogger-style pants gathered into ribbing at the hem. Front and back use the same pattern: sew the center (crotch) seams, then the sides and inseam. Elastic and a drawcord at the waist; ribbing sewn into a loop at each hem. Great in French terry.", shirtdress:"A button-front shirt dress. Two fronts, one back, two sleeves and a collar. A straight silhouette with no waist seam — belt it or wear it open as a layer. Lovely in linen or cotton.", jinbeipants:"Shorts to pair with the jinbei top. A generous rise with an elastic waist. Make them in the same fabric as the jinbei top for a matching set. The hems are simply folded and topstitched. Cool in crepe or seersucker.", kidsculotte:"Looks like a skirt, moves like shorts. The flared hem means no worries while running and playing. Front and back use the same pattern: sew the center (crotch) seams, then the sides and inseam. Elastic waist.", babyblanket:"A small blanket for the stroller or naps. Sew the outer and lining right sides together and turn. Layer gauze, or bind the edges with bias tape. Add an embroidered name and it makes a lovely baby gift. It is large, so if you set the corner radius to 0 you can skip printing and cut straight from the measurements.", babytoy:"A soft toy for a baby to grip. Sew two pieces right sides together, turn, then add stuffing and a bell. Catch a ribbon loop in the seam to clip it to a stroller. Backstitch the seams well so nothing can come loose.", beret:"A soft, round beret. Three pieces: top (large circle), underside (ring) and a headband facing. The top is larger than the underside, so it puffs up once joined. Try wool or corduroy.", hairturban:"A turban with a twisted front. Two bands cross at the center front, with elastic at the back for the fit. Stretch knit is easiest to put on. More decorative than a plain headband, and handy after a bath.", bottleholder:"A cylindrical holder that keeps a drink cold if you line it with insulating fabric. Base (circle), body and a shoulder strap. Great for summer outings and for school or nursery.", bodybag:"A compact bag worn across the body. Body (front/back), gusset and strap. The zip keeps everything secure, making it a good second bag for days out and travel. Nylon or canvas works well.", baginbag:"An insert that divides up a large bag. Body (base on the fold), divider and handles. Stitch the divider into sections to create pockets, then lift the whole thing across when you change bags. Use a firm fabric.", petmat:"A thin mat for a crate or a napping spot. Sandwich batting between the outer and lining, then quilt a grid so the filling stays put. Handy to have a few for washing.", petpouch:"A pouch for treats, waste bags and keys on a walk. Body (base on the fold), flap and a belt loop, so it threads onto a belt or bag. Choose fabric you can wash.", boxcover:"A fabric cover for a storage cube or box. One top piece plus four sides — a simple box shape. Swap it out and the room changes mood. Thread elastic through the hem so it stays in place. Every piece is a straight rectangle, so instead of printing you can mark the measurements from the guide sheet straight onto the fabric.", slipper:"Warm slippers that wrap the foot. Two soles and two uppers. Use non-slip fabric for the sole to be safe, and add batting to make them plush. Nice to make a few pairs for guests.",

    /* 2026-07 追加パターン2 */ poncho:"A layer you simply pull over your head. Just two pieces joined at the shoulders. Finish the neckline and hem with bias tape or topstitching. Lovely in wool, fleece or lightweight blanket fabric.", overall:"Dungarees with a bib and shoulder straps. Three pieces: bib, pants and straps. Front and back pants use the same pattern — sew the center (crotch) seams, then the sides and inseam. Denim or linen suits them well.", dolman:"A top where body and sleeve are cut as one, so there are no sleeves to set in. Just join the front and back — a great beginner project. The silhouette flows gently from the side seam into the underarm. Best in knit with drape.", kidstank:"A tank top with straps cut in one with the body. Only two pieces. Bind the neckline and armholes with bias tape or ribbing. Best in jersey knit.", kidscoat:"An A-line coat that overlaps at the front and closes with buttons. Two fronts, one back, two sleeves and a collar. The flared hem leaves room for layers underneath. Try wool or quilted fabric.", kidsbibapron:"An apron with a pocket that catches spills — just fold the bottom edge up and sew. Fastens at the neck with hook-and-loop or a snap. Laminate or nylon washes and dries quickly.", babycape:"A cape to throw over a baby while carrying them or in the stroller. Cut as a circle so it never pulls at the shoulders. Fasten at the neck with buttons or snaps. Try fleece or double gauze.", babypants:"Pants with a generous rise so a diaper fits comfortably. Front and back use the same pattern: sew the center (crotch) seams, then the sides and inseam. Thread elastic at the waist and hems. Knit or double gauze works well.", armcover:"Tube covers for sun protection or to keep sleeves clean. Sew the long edge into a tube and thread elastic at both ends. UV-blocking or cooling knit makes them great for summer.", keycase:"A small key case folded in half with a key ring fitted inside. Sew the outer and lining right sides together, turn, and fold at the center. Add the ring at the fold and a snap at the tab. Perfect for scraps.", glassescase:"A soft case with a flap that folds over and snaps shut. Fold one piece and sew the sides. Add batting or felt inside to protect the lenses.", sunhat:"A brimmed hat for sun protection. Three pieces: crown, side band and brim. The brim is cut as a ring; sew two together and turn. Make it reversible for two looks in one.", backpack:"A simple backpack with a flap closure. Body (front/back), gusset, flap and straps. A separate gusset gives it structure so it stands up. Canvas or heavy cotton works best.", roundkinchaku:"A drawstring bag with a round base that stands on its own. Two pieces: base and body. Make a casing at the top and thread cord from both sides so it opens easily. Also good as a lunch bag or cosmetic pouch.", petbed:"A round bed with a padded bolster around a flat base. Two pieces: base and bolster. Stuff the bolster so your pet can rest their chin on the rim. Make two for easy washing.", petcape:"A cape-style raincoat that covers the back. It fastens at the neck and belly with straps, so there are no legs to thread — easy to put on. Use water-repellent or laminated fabric.", petcollar:"A cover that slips over the collar you already use, so you can change the look in seconds. Sew it into a tube and thread the collar through. Make a few in seasonal prints — great for scraps.", pettoy:"A long stuffed toy a cat can hold and kick with its back legs. Fold one piece, sew, turn and stuff. Add catnip or a bell inside. Sturdy cotton or denim holds up best.", wallpocket:"A fabric organizer to hang on a wall or door. A backing panel plus pocket strips. Stitch the pockets into even sections to create separate compartments. Add loops at the top for a rod.", laundrybag:"A large drawstring bag that holds plenty of laundry. One body piece (base on the fold) plus cord. Make a casing at the top and thread cord from both sides so it closes firmly. Breathable cotton or linen is ideal.", chaircover:"A cover that slips over a chair back to protect it from wear and marks. Just two pieces joined together. Add ties or elastic at the hem so it stays put. Coordinate it with the chair pad.",

    /* 2026-07 追加パターン */ tightskirt:"A slim skirt that follows the hip and tapers slightly to the hem. Front and back are both cut on the fold (1 each). Intended for an elastic waist or a side opening. Add a 15–20 cm vent at the center back so you can walk comfortably.", pleatskirt:"A skirt made by folding the fabric into regular pleats. Cut two rectangles, sew the sides and thread elastic through the waist. The pleat count and fold amount are calculated for you. Crisp, lightweight fabric works best. Every piece is a rectangle, so instead of printing you can simply mark the measurements from the guide sheet straight onto the fabric.", wrapskirt:"A wrap skirt tied at the waist. One body piece plus two ties. It overlaps at the front, so it is easy to put on and forgiving about size. A generous overlap keeps it closed as you walk.", tieredskirt:"A full skirt gathered tier by tier, each one wider than the last. Three tiers, all rectangles, so it is straight sewing only. Every piece is a rectangle, so instead of printing you can simply mark the measurements from the guide sheet straight onto the fabric.", adultvest:"A sleeveless open-front vest. Two fronts plus one back. Close it with buttons or leave it open. Line it for a tailored finish, or bind the neckline and armholes with bias tape for a single layer.", cardigan:"A relaxed open-front cardigan. Two fronts, one back, two sleeves and front bands. Best in knit fabric. Make the bands from the same fabric and add buttons, or wear it open.", taperedpants:"Elastic-waist adult pants, roomy through the hip and tapering to the hem. Front and back use the same pattern: sew the center (crotch) seams, then the sides and inseam. Baste to check the crotch curve.", culotte:"Wide cropped pants that read like a skirt. The elastic waist makes them easy to wear. Front and back use the same pattern: sew the center (crotch) seams, then the sides and inseam. Fabric with drape works best.", jinbei:"A Japanese summer top for festivals or sleepwear. One back, two fronts, two sleeves and ties. The sides fasten with ties. Pair it with the kids' shorts pattern for a full jinbei set. Cotton, crepe or seersucker suits it well.", kidsrompers:"A sleeveless bodysuit that snaps at the crotch so it never rides up. Two pieces: front and back. Bind the neckline and armholes with bias tape. Stretch knit works best.", kidsraglan:"A T-shirt with raglan sleeves that run all the way to the neckline. Front and back bodice plus two sleeves. The diagonal shoulder seam makes sleeve setting easier. Use a contrast fabric for the sleeves for a classic look. Best in knit.", jumperskirt:"A pinafore dress with shoulder straps, worn over a tee or blouse. Bodice, gathered skirt and straps, joined at the waist seam with gathers.", kidshoodie:"A pullover hoodie. Front and back bodice, two sleeves and two hood pieces. Best in sweatshirt or French terry. Sew the hood pieces right sides together and attach to the neckline. Add ribbing at the cuffs and hem if you like.", sleeper:"A sleeveless sleep sack that keeps a baby covered through the night. Two fronts plus one back, opening at the front with snaps. Made in six-layer gauze or fleece it works all year round.", babyshoes:"Soft first shoes. Three pieces: sole, toe upper and heel. Add lining and batting for a plump finish. Lovely for warmth and photos before a baby starts walking.", babymitten:"Mittens that stop a baby scratching. One simple piece, sewn in pairs. Thread elastic at the wrist or tie with ribbon. Choose gentle fabric such as gauze or knit.", potholder:"A square pot holder with rounded corners. Sandwich batting or thick cotton between the outer and lining. Catch a loop at one corner so it can hang. Made to use as a pair, not as a mitt.", eyemask:"A soft eye mask. Add thin batting between the outer and lining to block more light. Silk or gauze makes a lovely lining. Catch the elastic in the side seams as you sew.", neckwarmer:"A tube that covers the neck completely. Just sew the long edge into a loop. Fleece or knit works best. Make it double-layered for warmth and to hide the seams.", maskcase:"A folding case to hold a mask you have taken off. Fold one piece of fabric and sew the sides. Add a divider inside to separate the mask in use from a spare. Washable fabric or laminate is handy.", ecobag:"A shopping bag that folds up small. One body piece (base on the fold) plus two handles. Lightweight, strong nylon or sheeting suits it. A base gusset keeps boxes and lunch containers upright.", bucketbag:"A cylindrical bag with a round base that stands on its own. Round base, body panel and two handles. The round base makes the contents easy to see and stops the load shifting. Canvas or another firm fabric works best.", curtain:"A plain curtain sized from your window, with hems and fullness calculated for you. Sew curtain tape along the top for the hooks. Usually made as a pair. The construction is the same for blackout or sheer fabric. Because a curtain is large, skip printing the pattern and mark the cut sizes from the guide sheet directly onto the fabric.", chairpad:"A rounded-corner pad for a chair seat. Sandwich batting or foam between the outer and lining. Add two ties at the back to fasten it to the chair so it will not slip.",

    tee:"A relaxed drop-shoulder box tee. Front & back bodice plus sleeves. Baste first to check the neckline and armholes before final stitching. Best for knit fabric.",
    apron:"Three pieces: trapezoid body, two waist ties and one neck tie. Waist-tie positions are shown as dashed lines. Add pockets as you like.",
    skirt:"A simple A-line (no darts) with both front and back cut on the fold. Waist is meant for elastic or a side opening. Cut one front and one back (2 pieces total).",
    flareskirt:"A circle skirt. Full circle gives maximum flare; half circle keeps plenty of volume while using less fabric. Intended for an elastic waist. The pattern is a quarter circle (fan) placed on folded fabric to cut.",
    mermaid:"A mermaid line: fitted from waist to below the knee, then flaring at the hem. Two pieces: upper panel (waist to seam) and lower panel (seam to hem). Cut 2 each front and back, 4 total.",
    sleevedress:"Three pieces: front bodice, back bodice and skirt. A simple A-line silhouette. Finish the neckline and armholes with bias tape.",
    adultgather:"The easiest gathered skirt (adult size): fold a rectangle at the top and run elastic through. Sew the two front/back pieces at the sides. Left edge is the fold (center). The gather ratio sets the fabric width and how full it looks.",
    widepants:"Relaxed straight elastic-waist pants. The front and back use the same pattern: sew center (crotch) seams together, sew the sides, then fold the waist over for elastic. Baste the crotch to check the fit.",
    halfpants:"Relaxed elastic-waist shorts for adults. Same pattern as the wide pants with a shorter inseam. Adjust the inseam for knee-length, above-the-knee, etc.",
    tunic:"A relaxed long top that covers to above the knee. Same drop-shoulder pattern as the T-shirt, with the sleeve length adjustable from three-quarter to full. Best for soft fabrics like knit, double gauze or linen.",
    camisole:"A simple camisole: sew the front and back at the sides and add straps. Both front and back are cut on the fold. Works with many fabrics (knit, satin, linen). Adjust strap length when worn.",
    blouse:"A front-opening button-down blouse. Back is cut on the fold; the front is cut as 2 pieces (including the button stand). Set-in sleeves. Best for woven fabric (shirting, double gauze, linen).",
    onepiece:"A waist-seam dress with sleeves. Fitted bodice and an A-line skirt. Best for woven fabric (cotton, linen, double gauze).",
    jacket:"A front-button jacket with set-in sleeves. Back is cut on the fold; the front is cut as 2 pieces (including the button stand). Best for thicker woven fabric (wool, cotton twill).",
    coat:"A front-button A-line coat. Back is cut on the fold; the front is cut as 2 pieces (including the button stand). Set-in sleeves. Best for heavy fabric like wool, tweed or quilted fabric. Adding a lining gives a cleaner finish.",
    kidstee:"The same drop-shoulder box tee as the adult version, sized for kids. Start from the height presets, then baste first to check the neckline and armholes. Best for knit fabric.",
    kidsdress:"A two-tier dress: a sleeveless bodice joined to a fully gathered skirt. Three pieces: front bodice, back bodice and skirt. Finish the neckline and armholes with bias tape. Close the back opening with a button or snap.",
    smock:"The classic pullover smock for nursery and preschool. A relaxed drop-shoulder body (shared front/back) plus tube sleeves. Run elastic through the neckline and cuffs to gather. Since it goes over the head, check that the neckline fits over the head.",
    kidsvest:"A simple front-opening vest. One back piece (on the fold) and two front pieces. Finish the neckline, front edges, armholes and hem with bias tape or facings. Close the front with buttons or snaps.",
    pants:"A relaxed silhouette using the same pattern front and back. Sew the center lines together, sew the sides, then fold the waist over for elastic. The right edge is the crotch curve (center seam side). Baste the crotch to check the fit.",
    kidshalf:"Shorts made by shortening the inseam of the elastic-waist pants. Same pattern front and back: sew center (crotch) seams, sew the sides, fold the waist for elastic. Hem the legs.",
    gather:"The easiest gathered skirt: fold a rectangle at the top and run elastic through. Sew the two front/back pieces at the sides. Left edge is the fold (center). The gather ratio sets the fabric width and how full it looks.",
    bloomers:"Soft bloomers with elastic at both the waist and the legs. Same pattern front and back: sew the center (crotch) seams together, then sew the sides. Shorter length and more ease give a rounder, pumpkin-like shape. For babies to kids.",
    swaddle:"A square body with a triangular hood at one corner. Place the outer and lining right sides together, sew around, and turn through the opening. Tuck the hood into one corner of the body.",
    bandanastai:"A cute triangular bandana-style bib. Sew the outer and lining right sides together, turn through the opening, and fasten the two neck points (the left/right corners of the top edge) with snaps.",
    stai:"The classic bib: layer the outer and lining, sew, and turn right side out through the opening. The neck curve is a guide; adjust to the actual neck size and fasten with a snap.",
    babyhat:"A tulip hat with a crown of 6 petals (gores) and a downward brim. Cut 6 petals; cut the brim on a double fold for one outer and one lining. Make it with separate outer and lining fabrics for a reversible hat.",
    legwarmer:"Leg warmers you just sew into tubes. Fold the long edge right sides together to make a tube, then fold and hem the top and bottom. Knit fabric is comfortable without binding. Cut for two (left and right).",
    kinchaku:"The simplest drawstring pouch: fold one piece of fabric at the base (bottom) into a loop and sew both sides. The dotted line near the top marks the drawstring casing (fold-over).",
    kincgusset:"A drawstring pouch with a triangular base gusset. Cut the base on the fold. After sewing the sides, pinch the base corners into triangles up to the notches (▽) to sew the gusset, then fold the triangles.",
    gymbag:"A drawstring-top bag. Cut as one piece with the base on the fold and sew both sides. Add loops at the bottom corners to thread the cord and carry it like a backpack.",
    shoesbag:"A shoe bag you can hang by its D-ring handle. Body cut as one piece with the base on the fold, plus one handle and one D-ring tab. Fold the opening, sandwich the handle and tab, and sew the sides.",
    movepocket:"A clip-on pocket that threads onto a backpack strap. Three parts: 2 body pieces, 2 flap pieces and 1 belt loop. Sew the body and flap right sides together and turn, then bind with the belt loop.",
    mask:"A flat mask with three pleats in the center. Sew the outer and lining right sides together, then fold the sides into elastic casings. The dashed lines mark the pleat folds.",
    fittedmask:"A fitted, shaped mask joining upper and lower pieces along a wavy seam. The curved seam creates the shape around the nose. Cut each part on the fold for outer and lining; sew each part, turn, then join top and bottom.",
    bandana:"A triangular headscarf worn over the head. Place the long edge across the forehead and tie at the back. Just triple-fold hem the three edges. Run elastic through the base edge to make it pull-on.",
    placemat:"A simple placemat sewn around four sides. Works either single-layer (folding the seam allowance as you sew) or double-layer (two pieces sewn and turned).",
    shuushu:"A hair accessory made by sewing fabric into a tube and threading elastic through. Length sets the fullness, width sets the volume. When joining the short ends, leave a small opening to insert the elastic.",
    headband:"A headband: sew the long edge into a tube, then join the short ends. Same construction as the scrunchie but wider. With knit fabric you can rely on the stretch and skip the elastic.",
    tissuecase:"A pocket-tissue cover made from a single folded piece. Hem the top and bottom edges (the opening), fold so they overlap at the center, then sew the two sides. The dashed lines mark the hems and center fold.",
    bookcover:"A simple wrap-and-fold book cover. Sew the top and bottom, then fold the left and right flaps (dashed sections) inward to tuck the book in and hold it.",
    bowtie:"The classic type: fold the body into a loop at the center, gather it, and wrap with a center band. Depending on size it suits adults, kids or dogs (threaded onto a collar).",
    tote:"A boxed-corner tote. The body is cut as one piece folded at the base (front and back connected). Cut out the squares at the base corners and sew to form the gusset. The handles are separate.",
    pouch:"A flat pouch with a zipper at the top. Simply sew the front and back together. The zipper length is roughly the finished width.",
    pouchgusset:"A flat pouch with a gusset made by pinching and sewing the base corners into triangles. Sew the front and back together, then pinch the base corners and sew along the gusset line (dashed).",
    gamaguchi:"Automatically sizes the fabric from the clasp size. Place the actual clasp above the dashed line to trace its shape and adjust the fabric outline. Fabric width is roughly clasp width x 1.6.",
    sacoche:"A simple shoulder bag made by sewing the front and back together. Close the opening with a zipper or magnetic snap.",
    azuma:"An azuma bag made by folding a single 1:3 rectangle into thirds and sewing. It wraps like a furoshiki for a bento or as a reusable bag. The dashed lines are the fold positions.",
    panel:"For cushion covers, bag panels, small items and more. Just enter width and height. Set one side as the fold to cut a symmetrical piece.",
    clutchbag:"A long-wallet-style clutch folded in half with the base on the fold. Add a zipper to the body or make a flap-over version. Fabrics with character (canvas, faux leather, cotton) work well.",
    shoulderbag:"A standard shoulder bag: sew the front and back body panels together and add a flap. Use an adjuster on the strap to change the length freely. Best for canvas or denim.",
    dog:"A tube-shaped tank sewn at the sides from a back panel and a belly panel. The front-leg openings are the inner curves at the sides. Body shapes vary a lot, so baste first and adjust to your dog.",
    dogsleeved:"A three-piece tank-style dog garment with tube sleeves added for the front legs. Adjust the sleeves to the front-leg girth. Front-leg shapes vary greatly between dogs, so we recommend basting the sleeveless tank first to check the back and belly panels before making the sleeves.",
    mannerbelt:"A belly band to prevent marking. Fold the fabric lengthwise into a tube, sew, and fasten with hook-and-loop. Finish it a bit longer than the belly girth and adjust with the overlap.",
    petbandana:"A triangular bandana that threads onto the collar. Fold the top edge into a tube to pass the collar through. Triple-fold hem a single piece, or make it from an outer and a lining.",
    petsnood:"To keep long-eared dogs clean at mealtimes or warm in the cold. A neck warmer sewn into a tube. Fold both ends over, or run elastic through one side to fit the face and neck.",
    catfuku:"The same tube-shaped tank (back panel + belly panel) as the dog clothes, adjusted for cats. Many cats dislike front legs, so baste first and adjust the front-leg opening position. The preset values suit a cat's slim neck and short body.",
    petvest:"A vest style with wider front-leg openings. Same pattern as the dog tank but with larger leg openings for a lighter silhouette. Just sew the front and back panels at the sides. Great for walks in hot weather or as indoor wear.",
    cushioncover:"An envelope cushion cover that opens at the back. No zipper needed and simple to make. One outer piece plus two back pieces. Best for sturdy fabric like cotton, linen or canvas.",
    tablecloth:"Just enter the table size and overhang to calculate the fabric you need. Simply triple-fold hem all four sides. Use any fabric you like — cotton, linen or polyester.",
    pillowcase:"An envelope pillowcase. No zipper needed; the overlap at the opening holds the pillow in. One outer piece plus two back pieces. Best for gentle fabric like cotton, linen or double gauze."
  };

